	VoidType

Expression is anything that has a type

Expression
	Cast
	MethodCall
		Explicit
		Indexing
		BinOp
		UnOp
	VariableAccess ( 'Name' | '.' 'Name' ) (points to an out of tree Variable. If analysis find that is is in fact an argumentless method call, replace)
	Constant
	Constructor
	MultiExpression (keep it a single object to avoid duplicating the list logic. Used for assignment RHS, call arguments, return values)
	ExpressionSelect (points at a multityped expression and has an index to chose which element. Used to wrap right side in assignments. Selecting in a MultiExpression should always return the smallest inner expression possible: "unfolding")

Variable (Something you can read/write. Created with declarations, pointed at by accesses and assignments)
	InstanceMember (has an object/expression, and points at Member)
	Local
		Argument

Statement
	(control structures)
	Expression
	MultiAssignment (the one we parse, then specialises into VariableAssignments and MethodCall's (for setter methods))
	VariableAssignment (points at separate Variable)

A multiassignment is split into individual assignments (variable or setter call). The right-hand side is split using individual expressions, or ExpressionSelect to split return values of method calls. Each sub assignment is self sufficient.
Method
Member
Scope
	Class
	Block

Scopes have lists of variables and can look them up. Scopes point at outer scope for recursive lookup

Arguments derive from Local Variables, and are stuck directly into the methods' bodies. Only their types are gathered at method level for interface building. The "self" local is added automatically.

Classes point at their parent, but inheritence is done only by lookup via Scope methods (no gathering of members and methods). At analysis, overriding methods and shared members get flags set.

For tree modification, all analysis functions are outside the object, so it's enough to pass a reference to the pointer to the node, to be able to delete the old one and replace it by a new built.
